WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.462
Halfway through the afternoon, the phone rings.

2
00:00:05.462 --> 00:00:07.637
The guests can't come: dinner is off.

3
00:00:07.637 --> 00:00:12.809
The cook turns the heat down, puts a lid on the soup, and takes the sauce off the stove.

4
00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.511
She heard the news between two steps of a recipe, when she glanced at her phone.

5
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:22.456
Her helper in the corner is picking small stones out of a big sack of lentils.

6
00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:24.515
He never looks up between lentils.

7
00:00:24.515 --> 00:00:27.481
So he hears nothing, and he sorts the whole sack.

8
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.784
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:33.932
When you cancel a coroutine, doesn't it just stop?

10
00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:35.443
How could it keep going?

11
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.705
It's natural to think cancel stops the code.

12
00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:39.464
It doesn't.

13
00:00:39.464 --> 00:00:42.364
It only asks, and the code has to look up.

14
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.831
Every coroutine has a job, the handle to its life.

15
00:00:45.831 --> 00:00:49.618
job.cancel() marks that job as cancelling, and that's all.

16
00:00:49.618 --> 00:00:53.275
It doesn't stop the thread, the worker running the code.

17
00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:01.071
The coroutine finds out at its next suspension point, a call where it may pause, such as delay, await or withContext.

18
00:01:01.071 --> 00:01:04.436
That's the cook glancing at her phone between steps.

19
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:06.805
Those calls look at the job first.

20
00:01:06.805 --> 00:01:12.403
If it's cancelled, they throw CancellationException, the exception that carries a cancel.

21
00:01:12.403 --> 00:01:16.868
Every finally block runs on the way out, and the job ends as cancelled.

22
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.286
Now the helper, in code.

23
00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:22.939
countStones goes through the lentils and counts the stones.

24
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:28.636
It's pure calculation, with no waiting, so there's no suspension point anywhere in the loop.

25
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:37.320
The lesson's demo gives four loops 60 million lentils each, on Default, and cancels each one 100 milliseconds after it starts.

26
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.198
The first loop has no check.

27
00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:41.815
How long after the cancel does it stop?

28
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.346
2,851 milliseconds.

29
00:01:46.346 --> 00:01:49.033
It didn't stop when it was asked.

30
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:51.881
It stopped when the sack was empty.

31
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.071
Very thorough.

32
00:01:53.071 --> 00:01:54.526
Not a great listener.

33
00:01:54.526 --> 00:02:00.141
So the point is: a cancel only asks, and code that never looks up never hears it.

34
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.114
The other three loops look up now and then.

35
00:02:03.114 --> 00:02:07.459
ensureActive throws CancellationException once the job is cancelled.

36
00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:11.611
isActive is a plain true or false, for a loop that stops quietly.

37
00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:14.806
And yield checks too, and lets others have a turn.

38
00:02:14.806 --> 00:02:18.256
They stopped 10, 4 and 1 millisecond after the cancel.

39
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:22.587
A check only reads the job's state, so it costs almost nothing.

40
00:02:22.587 --> 00:02:25.072
Once every thousand lentils is plenty.

41
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.926
Here's the loop with the check, in Kotlin, and its Java match.

42
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:37.199
Java's Future.cancel(true) works the same way: it sets the thread's interrupt flag, and the loop has to read it.

43
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.486
This is cooperative cancellation.

44
00:02:39.486 --> 00:02:43.280
Cancelling asks, and the code stops the next time it checks.

45
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.136
Now the cleanup.

46
00:02:45.136 --> 00:02:52.645
The cook is making jam in a big pan borrowed from the community kitchen, and the pan must go back, whatever happens.

47
00:02:52.645 --> 00:02:57.888
Giving it back means phoning the lending desk, so giveBack is a suspend function.

48
00:02:57.888 --> 00:02:59.636
It goes in a finally block.

49
00:02:59.867 --> 00:03:02.220
The jam is called off while it sets.

50
00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.592
Does the pan go back?

51
00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:06.770
No.

52
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:14.979
The finally block runs, but inside a coroutine that is still cancelled, and there, every suspension point throws at once.

53
00:03:14.979 --> 00:03:17.896
giveBack fails before the phone even rings.

54
00:03:17.896 --> 00:03:20.609
The community kitchen would like a word.

55
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:24.337
The fix is one wrapper: withContext(NonCancellable).

56
00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:30.468
NonCancellable is a job that is always active, so inside it, suspension points don't throw.

57
00:03:30.468 --> 00:03:37.408
The pan went back about 50 milliseconds after the cancel, and then the cancel carried on up, as before.

58
00:03:38.233 --> 00:03:39.279
Last, the timer.

59
00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:46.272
The cook sets a kitchen timer on the bread order: if the bakery hasn't answered in time, she bakes her own.

60
00:03:46.272 --> 00:03:50.586
withTimeout is that timer: when it runs out, it cancels the block.

61
00:03:50.586 --> 00:03:52.873
A timeout is a cancel with a timer.

62
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.159
The bakery takes one and a half seconds, and we wait 300 milliseconds.

63
00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:03.289
With delay standing in for the call, the timer works: 307 milliseconds.

64
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:08.873
But the bakery's old phone line blocks the thread until somebody picks up.

65
00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:16.062
Wrapped in withContext(IO), the timer fired at 300, and the caller still waited 1,519 milliseconds.

66
00:04:16.062 --> 00:04:22.016
The thread stayed on the line, listening to it ring, long after the timer gave up.

67
00:04:22.233 --> 00:04:24.218
runInterruptible fixes that.

68
00:04:24.218 --> 00:04:30.951
On a cancel, it interrupts the blocked thread, and the same call came back in 301 milliseconds.

69
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:33.184
Here the kitchen picture breaks.

70
00:04:33.184 --> 00:04:35.580
A cook can hang up whenever she likes.

71
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.435
runInterruptible can only interrupt, and that helps only if the call listens.

72
00:04:40.435 --> 00:04:43.588
Thread.sleep does; some old network clients don't.

73
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.058
So, does cancel just stop a coroutine?

74
00:04:47.058 --> 00:04:47.268
No.

75
00:04:47.268 --> 00:04:47.827
It asks.

76
00:04:47.827 --> 00:04:56.991
The coroutine hears it at its next suspension point or check, carries it out as an exception, and needs NonCancellable to clean up.

77
00:04:56.991 --> 00:05:00.348
And a timeout is the same request, with a timer.

78
00:05:00.567 --> 00:05:01.426
Quick check.

79
00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:07.082
A loop on Default weighs jars forever, with plain function calls and no checks.

80
00:05:07.082 --> 00:05:08.729
You call cancelAndJoin.

81
00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:09.660
What happens?

82
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:13.754
It never returns.

83
00:05:13.754 --> 00:05:15.995
Nothing in the loop ever looks up.

84
00:05:15.995 --> 00:05:19.224
Add ensureActive, and it stops at the next check.
